  "worked_solution": "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $n+1\\ge -1,$ that is to say, $n\\ge -2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,\u2026}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n-2\\ge -4,$ That is to say, $n\\ge -2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nB={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,\u2026}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A\u2286B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B\u2286A$ is true, while $B\u2288A$ is false. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $B\u2287A$ is equivalent to $A\u2286B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\".",
